gmap-395/module-4/tutorials/gmap-395-in-.description:
This lab aims to guide you through setting up Cinemachine in Unity's Universal Render Pipeline (URP), understanding its core components, and implementing dynamic camera controls
Unity 2022.3 LTS or late Basic familiarity with the Unity interface Open Unity Hub: Launch the Unity Hub application
Create a New Project: Click on the "New Project" button
Select URP Template: Choose the "3D (URP)" template
Name and Location: Enter a project name (e.g., "CinemachineIntro") Select a location to save the project Create: - Click "Create Project" Access Package Manager: In Unity, navigate to Window &gt; Package Manager Find Cinemachine: In the Package Manager, ensure "Unity Registry" is selected in the dropdown Scroll to locate "Cinemachine" or use the search bar Install: Select "Cinemachine" and click the "Install" button Cinemachine is a powerful Unity package that simplifies camera control, allowing for dynamic and complex camera behaviors without extensive coding
Cinemachine Brain: Function: Acts as the link between Unity's main camera and Cinemachine's virtual cameras. It interprets and blends the instructions from virtual cameras to control the main camera's behavior
Usage: Attached to the Main Camera Cinemachine Virtual Camera: Function: Defines specific camera behaviors, such as position, rotation, and follow targets. Unlike the main camera, virtual cameras don't render scenes but provide instructions to the Cinemachine Brain
Usage: Multiple virtual cameras can be created to achieve various cinematic effects, like cutscenes or dynamic following Select Main Camera: In the Hierarchy window, click on the "Main Camera"
Add Cinemachine Brain: In the Inspector window, click "Add Component" - Search for "Cinemachine Brain" and select it
Note: If you star by just creating a Virtual Camera (Right-Click -&gt; Cinemachine -&gt; Virtual Camera) a brain will automatically be added to the scene Main Camera.
Add Virtual Camera: In the Menu Bar, navigate to Cinemachine &gt; Create Virtual Camera
Configure Virtual Camera: A new GameObject named "Virtual Camera" appears in the Hierarchy Select it
In the Inspector window, you'll see the CinemachineVirtualCamera component Create a Target Object: In the Hierarchy, create a Cube (Right- click &gt; 3D Object &gt; Cube)
Position it at (0, 0.5, 0) Assign Follow and Look At Targets: Select your "Virtual Camera" In the Inspector, set the "Follow" and "Look At" fields to the Cube (drag the Cube from the Hierarchy to these fields) Adjust Camera Settings: Modify the "Lens" settings, such as Field of View, to achieve the desired framing Under "Aim", explore options like Composer to fine-tune how the camera tracks the target Play the Scene: Click the "Play" button to enter Play Mode
Observe how the virtual camera follows and looks at the Cube Move the Target:
While in Play Mode, select the Cube and move it around (or create a script to animate it) Notice how the camera dynamically adjusts to keep the Cube in view Cinemachine allows seamless transitions between multiple virtual cameras, enabling dynamic and cinematic camera movements in your scene.
Add a Second Virtual Camera: Navigate to Cinemachine &gt; Create Virtual Camera.
A new GameObject named "Virtual Camera (1)" will appear in the Hierarchy. Configure the Second Virtual Camera: Select "Virtual Camera (1)".
In the Inspector, set the "Follow" and "Look At" fields to a different target or adjust its Transform to a new position.
For example, create another Cube at a different location and assign it as the target. Cinemachine manages transitions between virtual cameras based on their Priority and Activation. Set Camera Priorities: Each virtual camera has a Priority property. The camera with the highest priority becomes active.
To switch cameras, adjust their priorities accordingly. Activate/Deactivate Cameras via Script: You can enable or disable virtual cameras through C# scripts to control transitions.
For example, to switch to "Virtual Camera (1)" when pressing a number key: using UnityEngine;
using Cinemachine; public class CameraSwitcher : MonoBehaviour
{
public CinemachineVirtualCamera vcam1;
public CinemachineVirtualCamera vcam2;&nbsp; &nbsp; void Start()
{
ActivateCamera(vcam1); // Start with vcam1 active by default
}&nbsp; &nbsp; void Update()
{
if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Alpha1))
{
ActivateCamera(vcam1);
}&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Alpha2))
{
ActivateCamera(vcam2);
}
}&nbsp; &nbsp; void ActivateCamera(CinemachineVirtualCamera activeCam)
{
vcam1.gameObject.SetActive(activeCam == vcam1);
vcam2.gameObject.SetActive(activeCam == vcam2);
}
}
```- Attach this script to an empty GameObject and assign **Virtual Camera1** and **Virtual Camera2** in the **Inspector**. Press 1 → activates Virtual Camera1.
Press 2 → activates Virtual Camera2.
Deactivates the other camera automatically.
Starts with Virtual Camera1 active by default in Start(). Customize Transition Blends: Cinemachine allows customization of the blending between cameras.
Select the Main Camera and, in the Cinemachine Brain component, adjust the Default Blend settings to control the transition style and duration. Cinemachine offers advanced functionalities to enhance camera behavior.
Add Noise to a Virtual Camera: Select a virtual camera (e.g., "Virtual Camera").
In the Inspector, expand the "Noise" section.
Choose a Noise Profile (e.g., Basic Multi Perlin) to add procedural camera shake. Adjust Noise Parameters: Modify the Amplitude Gain and Frequency Gain to control the intensity and speed of the shake effect. Play the Scene: Click the "Play" button to enter Play Mode.
Interact with your scene and observe the camera behaviors, transitions, and effects implemented. Refine Camera Settings: Based on the playtest, tweak virtual camera settings, noise profiles, and transition blends to achieve the desired cinematic experience. Info
By following this comprehensive tutorial, you've learned how to set up and utilize Cinemachine in Unity to create dynamic and professional camera systems for your projects.
For a visual walkthrough and more advanced techniques, consider watching the following tutorial:Video: How to use Cameras in Unity: Cinemachine Virtual CamerasThis lab aims to introduce you to post- processing in Unity's Universal Render Pipeline (URP), enabling you to enhance your game's visual quality by applying various effectsURP integrates post- processing effects using the Volume framework. Follow these steps to set it up
Select the Main Camera: In the Hierarchy, click on "Main Camera" Enable Post- Processing:
2. In the Inspector under Rendering, check the Post Processing box Add a Global Volume: In the Hierarchy, right- click and select Volume &gt; Global Volume Create a Volume Profile:
2. With the Global Volume selected, click "Add Component" in the Inspector
3. Choose "Volume" - Click "New" next to the Profile field to create a new Volume Profile
Now that the Volume is set up, let's add some common post- processing effects
Add Bloom: In the Inspector of the Global Volume, click "Add Override"
Select "Post- processing" &gt; "Bloom" Configure Bloom: Enable the Intensity property by checking its box and set it to 0.5 Adjust the Threshold to control the brightness level at which Bloom starts Add Vignette: Click "Add Override" again Select "Post- processing" &gt; "Vignette" Configure Vignette: Enable the Intensity property and set it to 0.3
Adjust the Smoothness to 0.5 for a subtle darkening around the edges Add Color Adjustments: Click "Add Override" Select "Post- processing" &gt; "Color Adjustments" Configure Color Adjustments: Enable the Post Exposure property and set it to 0.5 to brighten the scene Adjust the Contrast to 10 for more depth Modify the Saturation to - 10 for a slight desaturation effect After adding these effects, you should notice changes in your scene Bloom: Bright areas emit a soft glow Vignette: Edges of the screen are subtly darkened, focusing attention toward the center Color Adjustments: Overall brightness, contrast, and color saturation are modified To see the effects in action Ensure you're in Scene or Game view If effects aren't visible in the Scene view, click the "Gizmos" dropdown and ensure "Post- Processing" is enabled
Also ensure it is enabled on the Main Camera and in the URP Settings Asset Feel free to explore other post- processing effects Depth of Field: Blurs objects based on their distance from the camera Film Grain: Adds a cinematic grainy texture Lens Distortion: Simulates lens imperfections for creative effects To add these Click "Add Override" in the Global Volume - Select the desired effect
